<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	No App Sandbox. DupeFinder is distributed directly as a signed + notarized
	DMG, not through the Mac App Store, and needs unrestricted access to the
	user folders and system APIs it works against — none of which survive
	sandboxing. That's a deliberate distribution choice, not an oversight.

	Hardened Runtime is still fully applied at signing time: it's an
	independent protection (blocks code injection, forces signed-only
	executable code) that notarization requires and that doesn't need — or
	grant — Sandbox's restrictions.

	Every key below is explicitly false rather than omitted: these are the
	hardened-runtime exemptions this app deliberately does NOT take. Spelling
	them out means a future "just add an entitlement to make it work" change
	has to consciously flip one, instead of silently appending to an empty
	dict.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit</key>
	<false/>
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
	<false/>
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-executable-page-protection</key>
	<false/>
</dict>
</plist>
